NTMP.

23.
On Page 13 under Resident Participation/Education – Changed from 2 programs
“Neighborhood Speed Watch” and “Neighborhood Speed Patrol” into one new program
called “”Neighborhood Speed Watch Program” with the following text:
This Program is sponsored and prepared by the Cheyenne Police Department and City
Engineer’s Office. City residents record all observed speeds and report the speeds
back to the City. No formal citations or fines are issued.  However, the data collected
by citizens may be used by the Police Department to assign officers for selective
enforcement and by the City Engineer’s Office to evaluate posted speed limits and/or
evaluate the need for other traffic management tools.

24.
Table 2 of 4 on page 16 – Neighborhood Speed Watch Program allowed on Residential Local
or Collectors only.  Cells merged and text changed to “Only Residential Streets”.

25.
Page 23 Neighborhood Speed Watch Program rewritten to new language.
Residents concerned with speeding traffic in their neighborhood use this data
collection program to help determine if there is a speeding problem on a residential
local or collector street. Citizens agreeing to participate in the program are briefed on
the requirements and expectations of them. They are instructed in the use of radar
equipment, proper data collection and appropriate behavior while conducting the data
collection. A radar unit is loaned out to the citizens. This is typically a team effort;
one person in the group is responsible for operating the radar unit; another person
records the required information on a City of Cheyenne Neighborhood Speed Watch
Form.   Once all data collection is completed, the neighborhood residents return the
radar unit and all report forms to the Cheyenne Police Department Traffic Sergeant.

Questions?  Call Tom Mason, 637-6299 or tmason@cheyennempo.org
